{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2020",
    "useDefineForClassFields": true,
    /* `ES2022.Error` is added to an otherwise ES2020 program, and only that one sliver of
       ES2022: it declares `ErrorOptions` and the `cause` property, which is what makes
       `throw new Error(msg, { cause })` compile. ESLint's `preserve-caught-error` *requires*
       that form when a catch block rethrows with a new message, so without this line the lint
       rule and the type checker contradict each other and a catch block cannot be written at
       all — which is exactly what happened in `useDeckMeta`'s tag-read refusal.

       Safe at runtime rather than merely allowed: `Error.cause` is Chromium 93+, and this app
       is a WebView2 webview while the suite runs on Node. Raising `target` or the whole `lib`
       to ES2022 would also be safe, and is deliberately not done here — this is the smallest
       change that resolves the contradiction, and it keeps the emit target where it was. */
    "lib": ["ES2020", "ES2022.Error", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "skipLibCheck": true,

    /* Bundler mode */
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "jsx": "react-jsx",

    /* Linting */
    "strict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,

    /* Path aliases (no baseUrl: deprecated in TS 6, paths resolve from this file) */
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"]
    },

    /* This app is a webview: no `process`, no `Buffer`, and `setTimeout` returns a `number`
       and not a `NodeJS.Timeout`. Nothing in `src/` may be allowed to think otherwise.

       Empty rather than a list, and that is measured: the app relies on no ambient `@types`
       package at all. `vite/client` — which types `import.meta.env` and the `*.css`
       side-effect imports — arrives through the `/// <reference types="vite/client" />` in
       `src/vite-env.d.ts`, and a triple-slash reference is not gated by this option.

       Know what this does and does not buy, because the difference cost a review: it blocks
       the *automatic* inclusion of `node_modules/@types/*`, and nothing more. A transitive
       `/// <reference types="node" />` inside a dependency's own declarations walks straight
       past it — measured 2026-08-09, with `@types/node` merely present in the tree both
       `vitest` and `vite` pulled it into this program despite this line. **The fence that
       actually holds is that `@types/node` is not installed**; see `.storybook/node-url.d.ts`
       for the shim that keeps it that way. This line is the second lock, not the first. */
    "types": []
  },
  // `src` alone: `.storybook` is type-checked by its own `.storybook/tsconfig.json`, because
  // it is the half that needs Node types and this half must never see them (see `types`
  // above). `npm run build` runs both.
  "include": ["src"],
  /* The two files that belong to `tsconfig.sw.json` instead. They are compiled with
     `lib.webworker`, where `self` is a `ServiceWorkerGlobalScope`; in this program `self` is a
     `Window` and every line of `sw.ts` is an error. `swCore.ts` stays in BOTH programs — it
     names only types the two libraries share. */
  "exclude": [".claude", "src/pwa/sw.ts", "src/pwa/sw-env.d.ts"],
  "references": [{ "path": "./tsconfig.node.json" }]
}
